  "strategy": "Our approach is a dual-helix strategy that optimizes the sequence by evolving two complementary strands independently. The key areas are:\n\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start with a parent sequence if available, and generate multiple \"constant-step\" sequences with varying lengths (powers of 2). Each sequence is initialized with coefficients that are periodic but smoothed near discontinuities (where any large jumps could violate the problem constraints and increase score).\n\n2.Mathematical representation:\n   - Use a vector representation (a_array) where all sequences are normalized to unit sum for gradient descent optimization. The actual value vector will be reprojected to nonnegative values during evaluation.\n\n3.Search via GA (Mendelian-style):\n   - Operator 1: Crossover (swap portions with the parent normalized and rounded to the nearest candidate).\n   - Operator 2: Leading edge mutation (spiking at the beginning).\n   - Operator 3: Trailing edge mutation (spiking at the end).\n\n   - For each generation (every 0.1 seconds for heavy computations):\n        a) Mutate and crossover the entire population to explore new candidates.\n        b) For each candidate:\n            - Project to the simplex (normalize and clip to nonnegative).\n            - Evaluate using the authoritative function.\n            - Track progression and the best candidate.\n\n4.Overarching plan:\n   - Initialize a population of sequences (varying lengths, all normalized differently).\n   - Use a generation-based approach that alternates between GA operations and Adam polish.\n   - Monitor the score and search until the deadline.\n\n5.Search configuration (GA population):\n   - Population size = 512\n   - Generations = 1000 (checked every 0.1 seconds to balance budget)\n   - Choose powerful crossover and mutation points with exponential time scaling (each generation gets a fixed budget allocation).\n\n6.Computational budget and detection:\n   - Given 1800 seconds, allocate 1.8/5 = 0.36 seconds for display, 1.8/512 = approx. 3.5 seconds remains for computation.\n\n   - Implementation details:\n        def initialize_sequence(length):  # Creates a normalized version of our dual-helical approach\n        ...\n\n        During evolution, wait 0.1 seconds per generation to evaluate candidate scores and rotate the hill climber if necessary.\n\n7.Validation at the end:\n   - After completing the simulation annealing for the best candidate, output to the authoritative function and check the scores again.\n\nHowever, due to solver constraints (of a previous program), we often reuse the evaluator for detailed checks.\n\nLet's write the code accordingly, aiming for a balance between practical computation and informed search.",
